.TH PLICAD 7
.SH NAME
plicad \- the language of the sources of PliCad
.SH DESCRIPTION
Every source file of PliCad, a sheet metal part
.RI ( sm (5)),
a solid part
.RI ( part (5)),
an assembly
.RI ( assembly (5)),
a scene
.RI ( scene (5)),
a drawing
.RI ( draw (5)),
a table of materials and a file of parameters, is written in one
language, which this page gives; each kind of file adds its own
statements.
.SS Lines
A source file holds one statement on each line.
A
.B #
begins a comment, which runs to the end of the line.
A line that then ends in a backslash runs on into the next line: the
backslash, the blanks before it and the blanks that begin the next
line stand for one blank, and the statement takes the number of its
first line in messages.
Lines with nothing left are skipped.
.SS Words and numbers
The words of a statement stand apart by blanks.
A number is an expression without blanks, or one in parentheses:
numbers, names, the operators
.BR "+ \- * /" ,
parentheses and the functions
.BR floor ,
.BR ceil ,
.BR abs ,
.BR tan ,
.BR sin ,
.BR cos ,
.BR sqrt ,
.BR min ,
.BR max ,
.B hypot
and
.BR atan2 ,
whose angles are in degrees.
Any number may carry a unit:
.B in
for inches,
.B mm
for millimeters and
.B deg
for degrees.
A length without a unit is in mm, and an angle in degrees; what PliCad
writes, it writes in mm.
Every value must be finite and within 1e6.
.SS Names
.TP
.I "NAME = EXPR"
defines NAME, a letter or an underscore and then letters, digits and
underscores, as the value of EXPR, for the statements below it.
.TP
.BI include " FILE"
reads the definitions of FILE, a file of parameters: its lines
.I "NAME = EXPR"
and its comments, and nothing else.
FILE, as every file a source names, lies relative to the source.
.SS Places
The statements that place a part or move it share these words.
.TP
.BI move " DX DY DZ"
moves by (DX, DY, DZ).
.TP
.BI turn " AXIS DEG"
.RB [ about
.IR "X Y Z" ]
.br
turns by DEG degrees about the line along the world axis AXIS,
.BR x ,
.B y
or
.BR z ,
through the point (X, Y, Z), or through a point that the statement
says if it names none.
.PP
Steps of moves and turns apply in the order they are written.
The word
.B at
only gives a position, as in
.BI at " X Y Z" .
The two sides of a sheet are
.B near
and
.BR far :
the near side is the reference face, z = 0 in the flat.
.SS Materials and finishes
A material is the same statement in every source, and in a stream:
.PP
.RS
.nf
material NAME R G B ROUGHNESS DENSITY [draft DEG] [mark LETTER] \e
    [vdi GRADE | callout TEXT]
materials FILE
.fi
.RE
.PP
The color and the roughness run from 0 to 1, the density is in g/cm3.
A material is the body of a part, or a finish on its faces: a coat of a
sheet
.RI ( sm (5)),
or the finish of faces of a solid
.RI ( part (5)).
A finish may be the texture of a mold: a grade of VDI 3400, 0 to 45,
which reads VDI 3400 REF GRADE, or a callout, the rest of the line, as
.BR "callout MOLD-TECH MT-11010" .
A texture has no color of its own: its faces show the color of the body,
0.85 of it, and its R G B are unused; a coat shows its own.
.B draft
is the least draft that the finish needs, from 0 to 45 degrees,
which
.IR mold (1)
checks, and
.B mark
the capital letter that marks the finish on a drawing; without one, the
first from T on that no other finish of the drawing has.
A
.B materials
statement reads a file of material statements, by default from the
library,
.IR plicad-lib (7).
Two materials of one name must be the same.
.SS Names of parts
A name of a part, and of the features and faces of a part, is declared
by the statement that makes it, and the tools select by these names
alone.
Inside an assembly the name of a part of a subassembly is the name of
the subassembly, a slash, and its own name.
.SH EXAMPLE
.RS
.nf
# A plate and its flange, the flange as wide as a parameter.
include sizes.params
T = 1.5
sheet T 1 0.44
plate base 0 0 4in 50 at 0 0 0 axes +x +y
flange lip base +y 90 (W / 2) \e
    span 10 90
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
param(1), sm(5), part(5), stream(5), assembly(5), scene(5), draw(5)
